    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The Haas Center for Public Service at Stanford University has been instrumental in developing two key pedagogical frameworks, the Principles of Ethical and Effective Service and the Pathways of Public Service, which guide public service programming at Stanford and beyond. As we approach the tenth anniversary of Cardinal Service – a comprehensive suite of public service programs and opportunities at Stanford – we aim to more clearly define our goals and metrics of success by developing a center-wide student learning outcomes (SLO) framework. As part of my POLS project, I worked with Dr. Yi-Ching Ong, Executive Director of the Haas Center, and a working group of internal stakeholders to research, survey, and design a framework that reflects the current state of Haas programs. The process began with a literature review to survey how peer institutions conceptualize student learning outcomes for community-engaged learning, followed by discussion and ideation with Haas staff and students. This framework synthesizes and organizes input from staff and students on the current state of Haas programs and leaves room for further exploration of the aspirational goals that may inspire future Haas public service programming.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Muña, K. (2024). Designing our North Stars: Co-Creating a Center-Wide Learning Outcomes Framework for the Haas Center for Public Service. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/xk673zd0715. https://doi.org/10.25740/xk673zd0715.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
